
class Solution {
    public int getCommon(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {

        int i = 0; // pointer for nums1
        int j = 0; // pointer for nums2

        // Traverse both arrays until one of them ends
        while (i < nums1.length && j < nums2.length) {

            // If both elements are equal → common element found
            if (nums1[i] == nums2[j]) {
                return nums1[i];
            }

            // If element in nums1 is smaller → move i forward
            // because we need a bigger value to match nums2[j]
            else if (nums1[i] < nums2[j]) {
                i++;
            }

            // If element in nums2 is smaller → move j forward
            // because we need a bigger value to match nums1[i]
            else {
                j++;
            }
        }

        // If no common element found
        return -1;
    }
}
🧠 Intuition (Very Important)
Both arrays are sorted
So we don’t need nested loops
We move the pointer of the smaller element
This avoids unnecessary comparisons
⏱️ Time Complexity
O(n + m)
n = nums1.length
m = nums2.length
Each pointer moves at most once through its array
💾 Space Complexity
O(1)
No extra space used
Only two pointers (i, j)
⚡ Why This is Optimal?
Brute force → O(n * m) ❌
This approach → O(n + m) ✅ (much faster)
